{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: raw substring absence will keep producing false positives — scope it before slice 2 plugs live emission in**

`expectTextFreeOf` does a bare `text.contains(token)` over the whole surface. Today's red test is the proof (`jsr` legitimately appears for the wired `fa jsr` pass-through), and the catalog's token vocabulary is full of future collisions: `mcp`, `cube`, `sandbox`, `task`, `sqlite`, `lsp`, `dap`, `skills`, `approval` are all generic English/CLI words that appear in help prose for other features. Every hidden cell whose surface contains one of these will fail the same way the moment this helper is pointed at another real emitted surface (which is the stated slice-2 plan: "the assertions do not change, only the surface they run against").

